You are getting this exception after moving from Java 8 → Java 21 because Thread.stop() is deprecated and now effectively disabled.

In modern Java (Java 11+), calling Thread.stop() throws:

java.lang.UnsupportedOperationException

because it was unsafe and could leave objects in an inconsistent state.

Why Thread.stop() Was Removed

Thread.stop() could terminate a thread immediately, even if it was:

writing to a database

updating shared memory

holding a lock

This could corrupt application state.

So modern Java requires graceful thread shutdown.

Correct Approach (Java 21 Compatible)

Instead of Thread.stop() you should use:

A boolean flag

interrupt()

Fix Your Code
Old Code
ttransmit.stop();
Correct Way

Use interrupt().

public void stopTXDriver(){
    try{
        if (ttransmit != null) {
            Thread.State state = ttransmit.getState();
            logger.info("stopDriver():Transmit Thread:" + state);

            ttransmit.interrupt();   // graceful stop
        }

        if (pTX != null) {
            pTX.stopThread();
        }

        ttransmit = null;
    }
    catch(Exception e){
        e.printStackTrace();
        logger.error(e.toString());
    }
}
But Your Thread Must Support Interrupt

Inside the thread run method you must check interrupt.

Example:

public void run() {
    while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
        try {
            // your logic
        } catch (Exception e) {
            break;
        }
    }
}

OR

while (running) {
    if(Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()){
        break;
    }
}
Even Better Solution (Recommended for Java 21)

Use ExecutorService instead of manual Thread handling.

Example:

ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
Future<?> future = executor.submit(pTX);

Stop it:

future.cancel(true);
executor.shutdown();

This is the modern thread management approach.

Quick Summary
| Old Java 8           | Java 21 Replacement |
| -------------------- | ------------------- |
| `Thread.stop()`      | ❌ Removed           |
| `Thread.interrupt()` | ✅ Correct           |
| Boolean flag         | ✅ Safe              |
| ExecutorService      | ⭐ Best Practice     |


